# CHANGELOG

## 20260820

0.4.1: ~ .gitignore: + current default contents

1. ~ .gitignore: + current default contents


## 20260807

0.4.0: Add a CHANGELOG in the form changelogger writes, and retire the Changes section from the script header.

1. + CHANGELOG. Generated by changelogger from the revisions staged out of the git history, then edited. Tier 1 supplied 0.1.1 through 0.3.3 from their own Changes sections, tier 2 diffed 0.0.1 through 0.1.0, and tier 3 inventoried 0.0.0.
2. - The Changes section from the script header. The CHANGELOG holds that record now, and keeping both invites them to disagree.
3. The summaries for 0.0.0 to 0.1.0 are written here, those versions recording none. They are read off the diffs rather than off any prose, none having been written at the time: the five of them were made in a single day.
4. 0.3.1's second item is taken from the current header rather than from 0.3.1's own. It was written at 0.3.2 and filed under the 0/1 marker, which is to say retroactively, so generating each entry from the header of its own revision had it recording only the one item it knew of at the time. The fullest account of a minor series is in the newest header of that series, and wants checking against.


## 20260807

0.3.3: Make the script executable.

1. ~ bin/git-autobisect: mode 100644 --> 100755. A script carrying a shebang and not the bit to go with it cannot be run from a clone at all. Homebrew's bin.install had been setting the bit on the way past, so nothing showed until the formula moved to libexec.install, which keeps whatever mode the repository has.


## 20220617

0.3.2: get bisect /restart/reset/


## 20200320

0.3.1: Made the preparatory command optional as it should have been.

1. ~ most_recent_passing_test_file_hash(), so that the preparatory command is issued only if not nil.


## 20200317

0.3.0: Allow for optionally supplying a preparatory command line switch.


## 20190527

0.2.0: ~ test_file() to be able to cope with line numbers being supplied at the end with a colon separator.


## 20141219

0.1.1: The loop in most_recent_passing_test_file_hash() was prematurely being broken out of even if there was an unsuccessful execution of the test_command.


## 20141218

0.1.0: Remember the hash the run started at, and return to it at the end.

1. + starting_hash()
2. ~ most_recent_passing_test_file_hash()
3. ~ set_bisect_bad(): Use starting_hash() rather than reading HEAD again.
4. ~ set_bisect_good()
5. ~ main(): + a checkout back to starting_hash().


## 20141218

0.0.3: Name the bisect methods for what they mark, and put the usage in the header.

1. /set_bad()/set_bisect_bad()/
2. /set_good()/set_bisect_good()/
3. ~ most_recent_passing_test_file_hash()
4. ~ main()
5. ~ the header: + the three usage notes in place of the single command line.


## 20141218

0.0.2: Use system() and git rev-parse in place of Kernel#run and git log, and drop the check that HEAD is at the tip.

1. - require 'Kernel/run'
2. ~ set_good() and set_bad(): /run/system/
3. ~ set_bad(): /git log --format=%H/git rev-parse HEAD/
4. ~ main(): - the check for HEAD pointing somewhere other than the tip of the branch.
5. ~ the header: + a usage line.


## 20141218

0.0.1: Take the test command from ARGV alone, and run main() unconditionally.

1. ~ main(): - the test_command parameter and its default, ARGV being the only source.
2. ~ most_recent_passing_test_file_hash()
3. ~ set_good()
4. - the `if __FILE__ == $0` guard and the example command it carried.


## 20141218

0.0.0: Bisect to the most recent commit at which a test command passes.

1. + require 'Kernel/run'
2. + test_file()
3. + most_recent_passing_test_file_hash()
4. + set_good()
5. + set_bad()
6. + main()
